.TH "ltsp-build-client" "8" "2011-08-11" "" ""
.SH "NAME"
ltsp\-build\-client \- Builds an LTSP client system for use with the LTSP server 

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP 
\fBltsp\-build\-client\fR  <options>

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
\fBltsp\-build\-client\fR builds an ltsp client chroot using standard packages
for use with the ltsp server.  This chroot installation  can be modified in a
number of ways as outlined in the OPTIONS section.  If present, defaults will
be read from the configuration file /etc/ltsp/ltsp\-build\-client.conf, and
will be overridden by using the command line options. \fBltsp\-build\-client\fR
is usually run as the root user.  Visit http://www.ltsp.org for more
information.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP 
Not all options are available on all installations. For a complete list of
available options, use the \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-extra\-help\fR options.

.IP "\fB\-\-arch\fP \fIarch\fR" 10 
Builds a chroot with the specified architecture. Must be a supported
architecture of the distribution, and compatible with the host architecture.
Defaults vary by distribution.

.IP "\fB\-\-backports\-mirror\fP \fImirror_url\fR" 10
Adds a backports repository to the chroot, as an extra package source. The
value is a URL, optionally followed by a suite and components; with the URL
alone, \fIdist\fR\-backports and the components of \fB\-\-components\fR are
appended. Do not write a leading "deb", it is added for you.
.IP "" 10
Adding the source is all this does. Packages still come from the release
proper, because the backports suite is pinned at priority 101 \(em name what
should come from it with \fB\-\-backports\-packages\fR.

.IP "\fB\-\-backports\-dist\fP \fIdist\fR" 10
The suite to pull backports from. Defaults to \fIdist\fR\-backports for the
distribution being built, which is what Debian calls it.

.IP "\fB\-\-backports\-packages\fP \fIpackages\fR" 10
Comma separated list of packages to take from the backports suite rather than
from the release. This is how you take a newer kernel for hardware the stable
one does not cover yet, rather than importing a foreign one:
.br
\fB\-\-backports\-packages linux\-image\-amd64,firmware\-intel\-graphics\fR
.IP "" 10
Each name is handed to apt as \fIpackage\fR/\fIsuite\fR, and apt insists the
package really is in that suite. Naming one that is not gives
.br
\fBE: Release 'trixie\-backports' for 'ldm' was not found\fR
.br
which reads as if the suite were unknown \(em it is not, the package is simply
not in it. Until 6.3.10\-1 \fB\-\-backports\-mirror\fP on its own produced
exactly that: it defaulted to forcing ltsp\-client, ltsp\-client\-core,
ltspfsd, ltspfsd\-core and ldm out of backports, a list from the days when
Debian shipped LTSP 5 and none of which has been in Debian for years. It now
forces nothing unless you say so.
.IP "" 10
The components on the backports line are the ones \fB\-\-components\fR names,
so a package outside \fBmain\fR needs its component there too: firmware lives
in \fBnon\-free\-firmware\fR in backports as it does in the release, and is out
of reach without \fB\-\-components main,non\-free\-firmware\fR.
.IP "" 10
Name the package you actually want, never the \fBfirmware\-linux\fR metapackage.
It pulls \fBfirmware\-amd\-graphics\fR, 93 MB of firmware for AMD graphics
chips, onto a client that may well have none \(em against
\fBfirmware\-intel\-graphics\fR at 32 MB for the Intel side. On an image served
over the network to every client at every boot, that is not a detail.
.IP "" 10
A backports kernel also weighs more than the one in the release, and not only
because it is newer: Debian split the kernel package after 6.12. In trixie
\fBlinux\-image\-6.12.107+deb13\-amd64\fR is one 106 MB package holding kernel
and modules together. In backports \fBlinux\-image\-7.1.8+deb13\-amd64\fR holds
nothing at all and depends on \fBlinux\-base\fR (2 MB), \fBlinux\-binary\fR
(14 MB) and \fBlinux\-modules\fR (153 MB). The same kernel costs 62 MB more,
and a \fBlinux\-modules\-\fR package goes by in the build log that was never
there before.
.IP "" 10
One dependency changed with that split, and it matters more than the size. The
trixie kernel depends on \fBinitramfs\-tools | linux\-initramfs\-tool\fR; the
backports kernel depends on \fBdracut | initramfs\-tools |
linux\-initramfs\-tool\fR, dracut first. Backports packages are installed before
\fB\-\-late\-packages\fR, so at that point nothing has pulled
\fBinitramfs\-tools\fR in yet and apt takes the first alternative it is offered.
LTSP's initramfs hooks are initramfs-tools hooks. Ask for it by name:
.br
\fB\-\-early\-packages ca\-certificates,initramfs\-tools\fR

.IP "\fB\-\-base\fP \fIdir\fR" 10 
Sets the default path for the ltsp chroots to be installed into. Defaults to
/opt/ltsp/.

.IP "\fB\-\-chroot\fP \fIfile\fR" 10 
Sets the default name for the ltsp chroot. Defaults to the architecture
selected with \-\-arch or the running architecture.

.IP "\fB\-\-config\fP \fIfile\fR" 10 
Load configuration defaults from specified file. See \fBCONFIG FILE\fR below
for more information.

.IP "\fB\-\-exclude\fP \fIpackages\fR" 10 
Sets packages to exclude from the initial client chroot.
 
.IP "\fB\-\-extra\-help\fP" 10 
Displays all available commandline options.

.IP "\fB\-\-help\fP" 10 
Displays the ltsp\-build\-client help message.

.IP "\fB\-\-locale\fP \fIlocale\fR[,\fIlocale\fR...]" 10
Locales to generate in the image. A comma separated list; the first becomes
the image's default in \fI/etc/default/locale\fR. An entry may name its
encoding after a space, otherwise it is looked up in
\fI/usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED\fR.
.IP "" 10
Worth giving more than one. A stock image carries \fBC.UTF-8\fR and nothing
else, and in the C locale glibc refuses to translate anything at all \(em so
without this option no client can be switched to another language by any
setting in \fBlts.conf\fP(5), and \fBLANG\fR there fails silently rather than
complaining. Put the languages the fleet might want into the image once:
.br
\fB\-\-locale de_DE.UTF\-8,en_US.UTF\-8\fR
.br
and let each client choose with \fBLANG\fR. The list is accepted since
6.3.14\-1; before that the option took a single locale.
.IP "" 10
Without the option at all, the chroot inherits the build server's own
\fI/etc/locale.gen\fR \(em which is why an image sometimes has a usable
locale that nobody asked for.

.IP "\fB\-\-mirror\fP \fImirror_url\fR" 10 
Select the mirror to download packages from. file:/// and http:// URLs are
supported.

.IP "\fB\-\-purge\-chroot\fP" 10 
Purges the existing LTSP chroot if present.

.SH "CONFIG FILE"
.PP
\fBltsp\-build\-client\fR will read a configuration file to override built-in
defaults. the default location for this configuration file is
/etc/ltsp/ltsp\-build\-client.conf, and can be specified with the \-\-conf
commandline option.

.PP
values are NAME="VALUE" pairs, with # representing commented lines. most
commandline options have an equivalent. an example Debian based system with an
alternate base location, architecture, and distribution:

.PP
 # set alternate default location for ltsp chroot (\-\-base)
 BASE="/srv/ltsp"
 # build an amd64 chroot by default (\-\-arch)
 ARCH="amd64"
 # build a Debian sid environment (\-\-dist)
 DIST="sid"

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP 
